"use strict";(self.webpackChunkwebsite=self.webpackChunkwebsite||[]).push([[6895],{9273:(e,t,n)=>{n.r(t),n.d(t,{default:()=>s});n(6540);var o=n(4060),i=n(4848);function s(){return(0,i.jsx)(o.A,{title:"Tutorial Sysid",children:(0,i.jsx)("div",{className:"legacy-mars",children:(0,i.jsx)("div",{dangerouslySetInnerHTML:{__html:'\n\n\n\n<main class="container" style="padding-top: 100px; padding-bottom: 80px;">\n  <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 40px;">\n    <a href="/MARSLib/tutorials" class="back-link">\u2190 BACK TO TUTORIALS</a>\n    <h1>System Identification</h1>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>Instead of manually guessing your <code>kS</code>, <code>kV</code>, <code>kA</code>, and <code>kP</code> constants, we let mathematical regression do the work. MARSLib integrates natively with WPILib\'s <strong>SysId</strong> tool to algorithmically calculate the perfect constants for your drivetrain, arms, elevators, and shooters.</p>\n\n  <h2>1. The SysId Process</h2>\n  <p>SysId uses two distinct types of tests to build a State Space model of your mechanism. You will run each test in both the Forward and Reverse directions.</p>\n\n  <ul style="margin-bottom: 20px; padding-left: 30px; color: var(--text-secondary);">\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;"><strong>Quasistatic Test:</strong> Voltage slowly ramps up continuously at 1 volt per second. This precisely maps your mechanism\'s <code>kS</code> (Static Friction) and <code>kV</code> (Velocity constant).</li>\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;"><strong>Dynamic Test:</strong> Applies an instant voltage step (e.g. jump to 6 volts). This maps <code>kA</code> (Acceleration) and calculates optimal <code>kP</code> and <code>kD</code> gains based on inertia.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div class="callout callout-warning">\n    <h4>Data Logging</h4>\n    <p>MARSLib leverages AdvantageKit. All SysId tests automatically dump telemetry directly into the <code>.wpilog</code> file. MARSLib also features an Automated Log Uploader\u2014if the robot detects an active internet connection, it will automatically push your SysId logs to the team\'s GitHub repository.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>2. Running SysId</h2>\n  <p>Swerve Drives require their own tuning sequence because the wheels must all be aligned correctly first. We map the controller bindings to automatically trigger SysId in <code>RobotBindings.java</code>.</p>\n  <pre><code class="language-java">// Inside configureDriverBindings()\ndriver.a().whileTrue(swerveDrive.sysIdQuasistatic(SysIdRoutine.Direction.kForward));\ndriver.b().whileTrue(swerveDrive.sysIdQuasistatic(SysIdRoutine.Direction.kReverse));\ndriver.x().whileTrue(swerveDrive.sysIdDynamic(SysIdRoutine.Direction.kForward));\ndriver.y().whileTrue(swerveDrive.sysIdDynamic(SysIdRoutine.Direction.kReverse));</code></pre>\n\n  <h2>3. Mechanism Integration</h2>\n  <p>MARSLib\'s structural <code>LinearMechanismIO</code> and <code>RotaryMechanismIO</code> abstractions automatically have SysId hooked into them! You just have to instantiate a <code>SysIdRoutine</code> object in the Subsystem and pass it your IO methods.</p>\n\n  <pre><code class="language-java">SysIdRoutine routine = new SysIdRoutine(\n  new SysIdRoutine.Config(),\n  new SysIdRoutine.Mechanism(\n    (Volts volts) -> io.setVoltage(volts.in(Volts)),\n    null, // AdvantageKit logs natively\n    this // The subsystem\n  )\n);</code></pre>\n\n  <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--ai-cyan); background: rgba(41, 182, 246, 0.05);">\n    <h4 style="color: var(--ai-cyan);">Safety Notice</h4>\n    <p>Be extremely careful when running SysId on elevators or arms, as there are <strong>no soft limits</strong> enforced during SysId ramps (by definition, it disables PID restrictions).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2>4. Analyzing the Log</h2>\n  <ol style="margin-bottom: 20px; padding-left: 30px; color: var(--text-secondary);">\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Retrieve your <code>.wpilog</code> file from the USB drive or GitHub Actions.</li>\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Open WPILib VSCode -> Start Tool -> <strong>SysId</strong>.</li>\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Open the <code>.wpilog</code> file and select the mechanism under test.</li>\n    <li style="margin-bottom: 15px;">Drag your sliders to fit the feedback noise. The UI will instantly spit out your <code>kS</code>, <code>kV</code>, <code>kA</code>, and <code>kP</code> values!</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <h2>5. Continuous SysId (Online Estimator)</h2>\n  <p>What if the robot learned its own constants while the driver was practicing? MARSLib features an <strong>Online Feedforward Estimator</strong> built directly into its subsystems.</p>\n\n  <p>A zero-allocation, sliding-window Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression algorithm continuously monitors mechanism telemetry during normal Teleop driving. It dynamically computes empirical <code>kV</code> and <code>kA</code> constants in zero-GC real-time.</p>\n\n  <pre><code class="language-java">// Inside periodic()\ndouble currentVelocity = inputs.velocityRadPerSec;\ndouble currentAccel = (currentVelocity - lastVelocity) / 0.020;\nestimator.addMeasurement(inputs.appliedVolts, currentVelocity, currentAccel);</code></pre>\n</main>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n'}})})})}}}]);